## Pull Request: Refactor Commands and Implement Strategy Pattern

### Overview

This pull request introduces a significant refactor of the command structure within the `BookInventory` project, implementing the Strategy Pattern to enhance flexibility and maintainability. The changes encompass the command interfaces, the `DBCommandFactory`, the `BookRepository`, and the `BookRepositoryFactory`.

### Changes Made

The primary objective was to decouple the command creation logic from the factory, allowing for better extensibility when adding new database types in the future. This was achieved by defining a `ICommandStrategy` interface and implementing specific strategies for different database types.

#### Command Strategy Interface

A new interface, `ICommandStrategy`, has been introduced to standardize command creation across different database types:

```csharp
public interface ICommandStrategy
{
    IAddBookCommand CreateAddBookCommand(IDatabaseService dbService);
    IFilterBooksCommand CreateFilterBooksCommand(IDatabaseService dbService);
    ILoadingBooksCommand CreateLoadingBooksCommand(IDatabaseService dbService);
    IRemoveBookCommand CreateRemoveBookCommand(IDatabaseService dbService);
    IUpdateBookCommand CreateUpdateBookCommand(IDatabaseService dbService);
}
```
#### Concrete Strategies

The strategies for Access and SQL Server databases have been implemented, encapsulating the logic for creating commands specific to each database type:

```csharp
public class AccessCommandStrategy : ICommandStrategy
{
    public IAddBookCommand CreateAddBookCommand(IDatabaseService dbService)
    {
        return new AccessAddCommand(dbService);
    }

    // Other command creation methods...
}

public class SQLServerCommandStrategy : ICommandStrategy
{
    public IAddBookCommand CreateAddBookCommand(IDatabaseService dbService)
    {
        return new SQLServerAddCommand(dbService);
    }

    // Other command creation methods...
}
```

#### DBCommandFactory Update

The `DBCommandFactory` has been refactored to utilize these strategies, simplifying the command retrieval process:

```csharp
internal class DBCommandFactory : IDBCommandFactory
{
    private readonly ICommandStrategy _commandStrategy;

    public DBCommandFactory(DatabaseType dbType, IDatabaseService dbService)
    {
        _commandStrategy = dbType switch
        {
            DatabaseType.AccessDB => new AccessCommandStrategy(),
            DatabaseType.SQLServerDB => new SQLServerCommandStrategy(),
            _ => throw new NotSupportedException("Database type not supported")
        };
    }

    public IAddBookCommand GetAddBookCommand() => _commandStrategy.CreateAddBookCommand(dbService);
    // Other command retrieval methods...
}
```
#### BookRepository and BookRepositoryFactory Adjustments

The `BookRepository` class now integrates the updated command factory, maintaining a clean separation of concerns. The factory for the `BookRepository` ensures that the appropriate command strategy is utilized based on the database type.

```csharp
public static class BookRepositoryFactory
{
    public static ICreationResult<IBookRepository> CreateBookRepository(string connectionString, DatabaseType dbType)
    {
        // Existing logic...
        
        IDBCommandFactory dbCommand = new DBCommandFactory(dbType, dbService);
        repository = new BookRepository(dbCommand);
        
        return new CreationResult<IBookRepository>("Repository created successfully.", repository, true);
    }
}
```

### Benefits of the Refactor

The introduction of the Strategy Pattern allows for:
- Enhanced flexibility in adding new database types without modifying existing code.
- Improved readability and maintainability by encapsulating command creation logic within dedicated strategies.
- Easier unit testing of commands as each command can be tested in isolation.

### Conclusion

This refactor represents a significant step towards a more modular and extensible architecture in the `BookInventory` project. I look forward to your feedback and any suggestions for further improvements.

